---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Multi-App SSO">
  <h1>Multi-App SSO with One Identity Container</h1>
  <p>
    A common shape is "one product, several services" — a web app, a
    background worker, and an internal admin tool, all owned by the same
    company, all needing to authenticate the same users. Identity is
    designed for exactly this.
  </p>

  <h2>The plan</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One Identity deployment.</strong> One container, one user pool, one JWKS endpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>One front-end domain</strong> (e.g. <code>auth.acme.com</code>) hosting the login pages and calling Identity directly via Connect-Web.</li>
    <li><strong>N services</strong> each verifying access tokens locally against the same JWKS.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Identity deployment</h2>
  <Code code={`# In a shared "auth" cluster
docker run -d --name identity \\
  -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \\
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=entdb.svc:50051 \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=acme \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=acme.com \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=https://app.acme.com \\
  -e GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://app.acme.com,https://admin.acme.com,https://auth.acme.com" \\
  -e GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS="$(cat keyring.json)" \\
  -e GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)" \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:0.1.0`} lang="bash" title="identity (auth cluster)" />
  <p>
    Note <code>GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID</code> is the parent domain
    (<code>acme.com</code>): passkeys registered on
    <code>app.acme.com</code> are usable on <code>admin.acme.com</code>.
    Same domain rule applies to your front-end origins.
  </p>

  <h2>Front-end (Connect-Web)</h2>
  <Code code={`import { createPromiseClient } from "@connectrpc/connect";
import { createConnectTransport } from "@connectrpc/connect-web";
import { IdentityService } from "./gen/identity_connect";

const client = createPromiseClient(IdentityService, createConnectTransport({
  baseUrl: "https://auth.acme.com",
}));

const { accessToken, refreshToken, user } = await client.passwordLogin({
  email,
  password,
});

// Persist tokens in your preferred way (HttpOnly cookies on auth.acme.com,
// or in-memory + refresh on focus). Pass accessToken on subsequent calls
// to other services as Authorization: Bearer ...`} lang="typescript" />

  <h2>Service-side verification</h2>
  <p>
    Every service in the constellation verifies tokens against
    <code>https://auth.acme.com/.well-known/jwks.json</code>. There is no
    need to call Identity to check a token.
  </p>
  <Code code={`package authn

import (
    "context"
    "net/http"

    "github.com/MicahParks/keyfunc/v2"
    "github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v5"
)

var jwks *keyfunc.JWKS

func Init(ctx context.Context) error {
    var err error
    jwks, err = keyfunc.Get("https://auth.acme.com/.well-known/jwks.json", keyfunc.Options{
        Ctx:             ctx,
        RefreshInterval: 12 * time.Hour,
    })
    return err
}

func Authenticate(r *http.Request) (string, string, error) {
    raw := r.Header.Get("Authorization")
    if !strings.HasPrefix(raw, "Bearer ") {
        return "", "", errors.New("no bearer token")
    }
    parsed, err := jwt.Parse(raw[7:], jwks.Keyfunc, jwt.WithIssuer("identity"))
    if err != nil {
        return "", "", err
    }
    claims := parsed.Claims.(jwt.MapClaims)
    return claims["sub"].(string), claims["role"].(string), nil
}`} lang="go" />

  <h2>Logout everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    A user pressing "sign out everywhere" calls
    <code>SignOutEverywhere</code> on Identity, which revokes every
    refresh token. Existing access tokens stay valid until
    <code>exp</code> — typically 15 minutes. If your threat model needs
    instant kill, lower <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Per-service authorization</h2>
  <p>
    Identity issues a coarse <code>role</code> claim
    (<code>admin</code> / <code>member</code> / <code>guest</code>).
    Per-resource authorization belongs to each service: the admin app
    might require <code>role=admin</code>, while the public app accepts
    any authenticated user.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same boundary applies to groups: Identity can be the source of
    truth for group and membership data, but checks like "user must be in
    group X to access resource Y" still belong in the calling service or
    a separate permissions layer. Identity does not evaluate or encode
    those app-specific ACLs for you.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
